<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Waldeck</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1929 CE · capital Arolsen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Hessian republic, successor of the Principality of Waldeck-Pyrmont, which gave up its independence in stages to Prussia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Prince Friedrich of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-waldeck">Waldeck-Pyrmont</a> was deposed in November 1918, and the principality became a free state with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-arolsen">Arolsen</a> as capital. Waldeck had been administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> under an accession treaty since 1868, so its independence was already limited.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The free state was rural and poor, with farming and forestry in the hills around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eder-river">Eder</a>. Its finances depended on Prussian support. In 1921 the inhabitants of the detached district of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-pyrmont">Pyrmont</a> voted to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and Pyrmont passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-hanover">Province of Hanover</a> in 1922. The remaining state struggled to sustain its own government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the Free State of Waldeck was absorbed into Prussia on 1 April 1929 and became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-hesse-nassau">Province of Hesse-Nassau</a>. It is now in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-of-waldeck.jpg" alt="Free State of Waldeck" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Mmxx · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany_(3-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1929 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type<